Browser authentication uses an HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookie. Automation uses
`Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Only token digests and password hashes are stored.
{{if .Observability}}
Every response includes a validated or generated `X-Request-ID`. Access logs omit
query strings and bodies. `/readyz` waits at most two seconds for the database and
returns only a generic status; `/metrics` exposes aggregate process counters with
no user-derived labels. Protect these operational endpoints at the deployment
boundary when they are not intended for public network access.
{{end}}
{{if .Tenancy}}
Create an organization after signing in, then send its identifier in
`X-Organization-ID` for every protected business request. The generated stores
include the organization in reads, writes, uniqueness, pagination, and optimistic
locking predicates; a valid record identifier from another organization is still
reported as not found.
{{if .TenancyMembers}}
Administrators can create 72-hour email-bound invitations, change member roles,
and remove members. Invitation secrets are stored only as SHA-256 digests and are
returned once for delivery. Role changes and removals are serialized per
organization so the final administrator cannot be removed or demoted.
{{end}}
{{end}}
{{if .CSVTransfer}}

CSV import and export use exactly the mutable field order declared by the
Blueprint. Download the generated template before preparing an import. Documents
are limited to 5 MiB and 1000 rows; datetimes use RFC3339 and blank optional cells
become null. Import creates new identifiers and commits all rows in one database
transaction. One invalid or conflicting row rolls back the complete batch. Export
is audited and returns no partial document when its row or byte bound is exceeded.
Generated spreadsheet-cell escaping prevents formula execution and round-trips
through the matching importer.
{{end}}
{{if .Approvals}}

Approval submission verifies that the generated business record exists in the
current organization. A subject can have only one pending request. The requester
cannot approve or reject that request, and only the requester can cancel it.
Approve, reject, and cancel calls require the current decimal-string `version`;
successful transitions atomically append immutable history and a security audit.
The workflow records decisions but deliberately does not mutate the subject.
{{end}}
{{if .Catalog}}

Catalog products begin as drafts, can be published once, and can be archived
permanently from draft or active state. SKU uniqueness is scoped to the current
organization. Prices and optimistic versions are decimal strings in JSON, so
browser clients do not lose `int64` precision. Public routes require no login but
return active products only; tenant projects require `X-Organization-ID` and hide
products while that organization is inactive.
{{end}}
{{if .CustomerAccounts}}

Storefront customer accounts do not use staff roles, staff API tokens, or the
`scaffold_session` cookie. Their raw 30-day token is returned only as the
`scaffold_customer_session` HttpOnly cookie, while the database stores SHA-256
digests. Password changes, self-closure, suspension, and reactivation revoke all
customer sessions. Closed accounts are retained as terminal principals for
future order references. This version intentionally does not generate email
verification or password recovery without a notification-dependent capability.
{{end}}
{{if .CRM}}

Business accounts, contacts, immutable activities, and forward-only sales
opportunities are available under `/api/v1/crm`. Business accounts are not
tenancy organizations, and contacts are not staff or storefront identities.
Amounts and optimistic versions cross JSON as decimal strings. Use `crm:read`
and `crm:write` for ordinary records, and reserve `crm:pipeline:manage` for stage
changes. Opportunities advance through `lead`, `qualified`, `proposal`, and
`won`; any nonterminal stage may become `lost`. Every mutation commits its audit
event atomically, and inactive tenant organizations cannot read or write CRM.
{{end}}
{{if .Inventory}}

Inventory quantities and optimistic versions cross JSON as decimal strings and
never use floating point. `on_hand`, `reserved`, and `available` are changed only
inside store transactions that append an immutable movement and security audit.
Idempotency keys persist the first result snapshot, so safe retries cannot apply
stock twice. Reservations move once from `active` to `released` or `consumed`.
Purchase orders move from `draft` to `submitted`, then through partial receipt to
`received`; an order with received stock cannot be cancelled. Archived items and
warehouses remain available to history but reject new stock operations.
{{end}}
